<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST139"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Weeza<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Weeza<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, May 30, 2004 - 1:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->‘All that is necessary for the triumph of evil is that good men do nothing’  <BR>— Edmund Burke <BR><a href="http://www.wm3.org" target=_top>www.wm3.org</a> <BR>  <BR>Join me at - Cold Cases Discussion Group <BR><a href="http://groups.yahoo.com/group/coldcases/" target=_top>http://groups.yahoo.com/group/coldcases/</a> <BR> <BR>Cold cases is a volunteer run investigation group. Most of their cases are gathering dust on police departments shelves, so I volunteer my time along with hundreds of others in an attempt to give the missing and unidentified back their names. You do have to apply to join though. <BR>Their sister site is <BR><a href="http://www.doenetwork.us" target=_top>www.doenetwork.us</a> <BR> <BR>The WM3 case is one I feel strongly about, so please visit their site to learn more.DNA tests are pending in the case.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
